---
import Site from '../../../layouts/Site.astro';
import StudyHeader from '../../../components/StudyHeader.astro';
import DecisionBrief from '../../../components/DecisionBrief.astro';
import StudyEvidence from '../../../components/StudyEvidence.astro';
import AirlineExplorer from '../../../components/AirlineExplorer';
import { studies } from '../../../data/studies';
import airline from '../../../data/airline-overview.json';
import '../../../styles/allocation-studies.css';
const reference=airline.default_case;
const differences=airline.scenarios.map(s=>s.baseline.total_fleet-s.optimized.total_fleet);
const fmt=(v:number,d=0)=>new Intl.NumberFormat('en-GB',{maximumFractionDigits:d}).format(v);
---
<Site title={studies.airline.title} description="Explore airline fleet composition with open BTS data: whole aircraft counts, route coverage and operating-hour limits across a retrospective 2025 planning envelope.">
 <StudyHeader study={studies.airline}/>
 <DecisionBrief id="airline-model" title="The fleet composition decision" items={[
  {label:'The decision',text:'Choose how many aircraft of each of three types the planning fleet needs. Assign whole paired round trips to eligible route/type combinations each month. The aircraft counts must cover the entire year.'},
  {label:'The objective',text:'First minimize total aircraft. Among minimum-fleet solutions, minimize annual round trips, then model aircraft minutes. Acquisition prices, fuel costs and profit are not in the objective.'},
  {label:'The information',text:'Use 2025 BTS records of performed departures, seats, carried passengers and ramp-to-ramp minutes. All twelve months are known in this retrospective exercise; the targets are not forecasts of future or unmet demand.'},
  {label:'The constraints',text:'Every selected route and month must meet its passenger seat-capacity target and minimum frequency. Each aircraft type has a monthly hour envelope. Only sufficiently observed route/type pairs are eligible.'},
 ]}>
  <p class="model-control-explanation"><strong>Service targets and aircraft hours are scenario inputs.</strong> You choose passenger coverage, minimum frequency and a daily hour allowance. The model chooses fleet counts and service assignments. Two 30-minute turnarounds per round trip and a 10% reserve are fixed hypothetical assumptions, not measured SkyWest practices.</p>
  <details class="data-details model-formulation"><summary>Variables, objective and comparison rule</summary><p>Let integer <var>n</var><sub>t</sub> count aircraft of type <var>t</var>, and integer <var>x</var><sub>rtm</sub> count round trips on route <var>r</var> in month <var>m</var>. Seat coefficients use the floored smaller directional average; block minutes sum the rounded-up directional averages.</p><ul class="model-equations"><li>∑<sub>t</sub> seats<sub>rt</sub> × x<sub>rtm</sub> ≥ passenger target<sub>rm</sub></li><li>∑<sub>t</sub> x<sub>rtm</sub> ≥ frequency floor<sub>rm</sub></li><li>∑<sub>r</sub> (block minutes<sub>rt</sub> + 60) × x<sub>rtm</sub> ≤ n<sub>t</sub> × available minutes<sub>m</sub></li><li>n<sub>t</sub>, x<sub>rtm</sub> are nonnegative integers.</li></ul><p>A paired round trip supplies seats in both directions; targets use the larger directional passenger and departure totals. Available minutes equal calendar days × daily hours × 60 × 90%, rounded down. There is no within-day aircraft circulation model.</p><p>The historical-share rule allocates round trips approximately in each route’s observed annual type proportions, using exact-total largest-remainder rounding. It adds service until both targets are met, then sizes a sufficient fleet by type. Both policies use the same targets, eligible pairs and aircraft-hour constraints. The rule is a reproducible baseline, not SkyWest’s actual fleet.</p><p>Three integer linear optimization stages establish the objective levels. Every scenario requires optimal solver termination with zero reported gap and exact integer checks of all constraints. Multiple fleet compositions can attain the same objectives.</p></details>
  <p class="model-scope"><strong>Scope:</strong> eight regional routes connected to Denver, operated in the source by SkyWest. This is an aggregate capacity-planning study. Tail scheduling, crew, maintenance, airport/range certification, available inventory and commercial contracts are not represented; no executable airline schedule or procurement recommendation is claimed.</p>
 </DecisionBrief>
 <section class="section tint" id="airline-explorer"><div class="shell"><div class="explorer-intro"><h2>Build the service envelope. Inspect the fleet.</h2><p>Change a service requirement or aircraft-hour allowance and see where the fleet mix moves. Then set a cap to identify when the service plan becomes impossible within the model.</p><p><a href="#airline-model">Review the decision and assumptions ↑</a></p></div><AirlineExplorer index={airline} client:load/><noscript><p>The reference scenario and complete scenario summary are shown. Enable JavaScript to change assumptions, month detail and the fleet cap, or use the Colab notebook below.</p></noscript><p class="source-credit">{airline.source.credit}</p></div></section>
 <section class="section shell study-prose" id="findings"><p class="eyebrow">What the evidence supports</p><h2>Fleet size depends on the service you insist on keeping.</h2><p>The predefined reference covers 100% of recorded carried passengers with at least 75% of recorded departures and an eight-hour daily allowance before the reserve. It needs {reference.optimized.total_fleet} model aircraft, compared with {reference.baseline.total_fleet} under the historical-share rule. One minimum mix uses {reference.optimized.fleet_by_type['629']} of BTS type 629, {reference.optimized.fleet_by_type['631']} of type 631 and {reference.optimized.fleet_by_type['673']} of type 673.</p><p>The minimum plan schedules {fmt(reference.optimized.total_roundtrips)} paired round trips versus {fmt(reference.baseline.total_roundtrips)} for the rule. Across all 27 settings, the model fleet difference ranges from {Math.min(...differences)} to {Math.max(...differences)} aircraft. These are differences between constructed planning policies under the same simplified constraints; they are not excess aircraft identified in the airline’s actual fleet.</p><p>At the same passenger and frequency targets, the six-, eight- and ten-hour assumptions require {airline.scenarios.filter(s=>s.parameters.passenger_multiplier===1&&s.parameters.frequency_fraction===.75).map(s=>s.optimized.total_fleet).join(', ')} aircraft respectively. This sensitivity makes the missing operational detail consequential: an hour envelope is not a guarantee that tails can operate the implied rotations.</p><div class="scope-note"><p>Fewer model aircraft need not mean a better commercial decision. Fleet ownership and lease costs, passenger preference for frequency, connection schedules, aircraft availability and qualified crews could change the preferred mix. The model identifies an aggregate capacity trade-off to investigate.</p></div></section>
 <section class="section tint" id="method"><div class="shell study-prose"><p class="eyebrow">Method &amp; preparation</p><h2>Keep capacity, service and evidence distinct.</h2><div class="method-grid"><article><h3>A bounded route portfolio</h3><p>The original 2025 export contains 455,857 rows. The carrier/hub passenger-service subset has 3,956 rows; the fixed-type, non-self-route scope has 3,845. The eight highest-passenger eligible routes have positive service in both directions in every month and at least two admitted types.</p></article><article><h3>Observed compatibility</h3><p>The model admits 23 route/type pairs with at least twelve performed departures in each direction over the year. This is evidence of observed use, not an independent certification check. Numeric BTS type and airport IDs remain the keys.</p></article><article><h3>Full-year information</h3><p>The route selection, coefficients and targets use the full historical year. No forecasting or held-out performance claim is made. All 27 scenarios and 81 optimization stages are retained, with whole-aircraft and whole-round-trip feasibility verified.</p></article></div><details class="data-details"><summary>What the aggregate source cannot tell us</summary><p>Carried passengers are fulfilled traffic records, not latent demand. Monthly seat and time averages do not reveal an aircraft’s location at each moment, a precise cabin configuration or a daily timetable. Pairing the two directions and using the larger requirement can add capacity relative to the observed asymmetric service. The source does not identify spare tails, acquisition cost, revenue or crew availability.</p><p>The optional fleet cap compares against each policy’s required count. If the cap is below the certified minimum, no solution exists within this model. A cap may admit the optimized plan while excluding the historical-share rule; that distinction remains visible in the tool.</p></details></div></section>
 <StudyEvidence title="Open flight records. Reproducible fleet choices." notebook="airline"><Fragment slot="description"><p>{airline.source.credit} The notebook loads the exact original-value carrier/hub subset, preparation code, protocol, model, source register, tests and complete results. The full BTS export is identified by its retrieval details and hash.</p></Fragment><p><a href={airline.source.url}>Original BTS dataset</a> · <a href="https://www.transportation.gov/sites/dot.gov/files/docs/mission/128511/policy-data-management-s10-160926-001-d5-071317.pdf">DOT data reuse policy</a></p></StudyEvidence>
</Site>
